WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.257
Picture the café where this course began.

2
00:00:05.257 --> 00:00:14.425
Each card payment becomes one line in the payments app, and a program turns those lines into the owner's takings: the money her shop took each day.

3
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:16.712
Now finance asks for one change.

4
00:00:16.712 --> 00:00:21.763
Takings should subtract refunds, and count each payment on the day it happened.

5
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:27.380
A month of numbers must be worked out again, while two thousand owners keep looking at theirs.

6
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.452
How do you redo a month without anybody seeing a hole?

7
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:31.806
Here's the setup.

8
00:00:31.806 --> 00:00:36.426
The log is every payment, one after another, in the order they arrived.

9
00:00:36.426 --> 00:00:38.379
Nothing in it is ever changed.

10
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:44.769
A job called v1 reads each new line as it lands, and adds it to a table: takings, by day.

11
00:00:44.769 --> 00:00:47.457
The owners' dashboards read that table.

12
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:53.527
The tempting plan: change v1's code, empty the table, and let it fill up again.

13
00:00:53.527 --> 00:00:55.233
One program, one table.

14
00:00:55.233 --> 00:00:56.049
Feels tidy.

15
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.430
But until it refills, every owner sees a month with holes in it.

16
00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:01.527
That's not a fix.

17
00:01:01.527 --> 00:01:03.979
That's an outage with good intentions.

18
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:05.980
The café already knows better.

19
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:09.706
Picture the clerk who keeps the takings sheet from the notebook.

20
00:01:09.706 --> 00:01:14.131
When her method changes, she doesn't rub out the sheet the owner is reading.

21
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.890
She takes a fresh sheet, starts from the notebook's first page, and works forward.

22
00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.285
When she's caught up, she hands it over, and bins the old one.

23
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:23.107
Map it.

24
00:01:23.107 --> 00:01:24.959
The notebook is the log.

25
00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:26.734
The sheet is the table.

26
00:01:26.734 --> 00:01:28.972
Her method is the job's code.

27
00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:35.680
And here the picture breaks in our favour: a program rereads a month in minutes, and can do it again tomorrow.

28
00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:38.989
So start a second job, v2, with the new code.

29
00:01:38.989 --> 00:01:43.557
It reads the log from the very first line and writes to a new table.

30
00:01:43.557 --> 00:01:45.437
Nobody reads that table yet.

31
00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:51.682
Now suppose v2 kept one habit from v1: it puts each payment on the day it handled it.

32
00:01:51.682 --> 00:01:53.817
For a year, that looked right.

33
00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:56.941
Where will this replay put the month's takings?

34
00:01:56.941 --> 00:01:57.920
Pause and guess.

35
00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:03.743
All of it on today.

36
00:02:03.743 --> 00:02:10.747
554,322.25 on day 30, and zero on every day before.

37
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.627
According to this table, the café sold a month of coffee this morning.

38
00:02:15.627 --> 00:02:16.468
Busy morning.

39
00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:18.625
The habit was wrong all along.

40
00:02:18.625 --> 00:02:24.508
Live, a payment is handled within a second of happening, so the two days almost always matched.

41
00:02:24.508 --> 00:02:29.587
A replay handles every line today, so the hidden mistake becomes the whole answer.

42
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:33.452
The day the job handles a line is called processing time.

43
00:02:33.452 --> 00:02:38.594
The day written inside the line, when the payment happened, is called event time.

44
00:02:38.594 --> 00:02:40.435
A replay must use event time.

45
00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:45.425
Replay again, by event time.

46
00:02:45.425 --> 00:02:48.315
Day 0: 19,684.20.

47
00:02:48.315 --> 00:02:51.204
Day 1: 19,139.78.

48
00:02:51.433 --> 00:02:54.939
And so on, up to today, which has only just begun.

49
00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:57.263
How long does it take?

50
00:02:57.263 --> 00:03:03.634
v2 reads 400 lines a minute while 5 new ones arrive, so it gains 395 a minute.

51
00:03:03.634 --> 00:03:07.391
Six thousand lines of history take 16 minutes.

52
00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:12.845
Until then, v2's table is missing the recent days, so nobody moves to it.

53
00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:19.433
When v2 reaches the end of the log, both tables follow the same lines, each with its own code.

54
00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.557
Now switch the owners to v2.

55
00:03:21.557 --> 00:03:26.135
Nobody saw a hole, because the old table kept serving the whole time.

56
00:03:26.367 --> 00:03:29.253
Later, stop v1 and delete its table.

57
00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.208
That's the whole recipe.

58
00:03:31.208 --> 00:03:41.737
Jay Kreps, who helped build Kafka, a program that keeps logs like this one, suggested it in 2014, and half joking, called it the kappa architecture.

59
00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:43.800
One engine, one codebase.

60
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.615
Replaying the oldest part of the log is the batch job.

61
00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.977
It works under three conditions.

62
00:03:49.977 --> 00:03:53.351
The replay runs through the same code as live lines.

63
00:03:53.351 --> 00:03:56.336
A crash halfway must not count anything twice.

64
00:03:56.336 --> 00:03:59.127
And every grouping by time uses event time.

65
00:03:59.367 --> 00:04:00.342
Then the bill.

66
00:04:00.342 --> 00:04:02.710
The log must still hold the month.

67
00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:09.328
Kafka deletes lines older than 7 days unless you tell it otherwise, so keep 30 days on purpose.

68
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.109
A busy log holds 130 billion lines after a month.

69
00:04:13.109 --> 00:04:17.763
And while both versions run, you pay for two jobs and two tables.

70
00:04:18.033 --> 00:04:24.472
So: keep the log, replay it into a second table by event time, and switch the readers when it has caught up.

71
00:04:24.472 --> 00:04:26.857
Never rebuild the table they're reading.

72
00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.570
One quick check.

73
00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:32.281
You replay at 100 lines a minute instead of 400.

74
00:04:32.281 --> 00:04:34.678
What changes, and what doesn't?

75
00:04:34.678 --> 00:04:35.915
Pause and think.

76
00:04:39.167 --> 00:04:41.463
It takes 64 minutes instead of 16.

77
00:04:41.463 --> 00:04:44.503
And the daily takings don't change by a cent.

78
00:04:44.503 --> 00:04:48.488
The answer depends on the log, not on how fast you read it.
